  "preamble": "The voiceless lateral affricate [tɬ] loses its stop closure and voicelessness to become the plain lateral [l]. In the Nahuan languages the /tɬ/ created by Whorf's law (Proto-Uto-Aztecan *t before *a) became /l/ in several dialects, notably Michoacán Nahuatl, alongside the eastern and southern change to /t/; Mexican Spanish shows the same outcome in a few loanwords with final -tl.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lenition, Deaffrication, Voicing",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Michoacán Nahuatl",
      "languageFamily": "Nahuan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Nahuan *tlaakatl 'man' (Central Nahuatl tlacatl)",
          "to": "lacal",
          "note": "Both the initial and the final (absolutive) /tɬ/ appear as /l/; Michoacán Nahuatl has no /tɬ/ phoneme."
        },
        {
          "from": "Classical Nahuatl tlaxcalli 'tortilla'",
          "to": "laxcali",
          "note": "Compare Morelos Nahuatl tlaxkale and Tetelcingo Nahuatl tlaxcali̱, which keep tl."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Nahuan *šoočɨ-tl 'flower' (Classical xochitl)",
          "to": "xuchil",
          "note": "The absolutive suffix -tl surfaces as -l."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Nahuan *ȼapo-tl 'sapote'",
          "to": "tzapol",
          "note": "Same treatment of the absolutive -tl."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Mexican Spanish",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Classical Nahuatl cempōhualxōchitl 'marigold'",
          "to": "cempasúchil",
          "note": "Word-final [tɬ] adapted as [l]; more often Spanish gave -te (elotl > elote, petatl > petate)."
        },
        {
          "from": "Classical Nahuatl xōchitl 'flower'",
          "to": "xóchil, súchil (poetic, dialectal)",
          "note": "Same adaptation of final -tl."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
